      <workList xml:id="workDesc_N2035D">
         <work xml:id="work_d1e225222">
            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_N20364">B2:425</identifier>
            <title xml:id="_19" xml:lang="da">Klage-Sang over den stormægtigste Monark, nu hos Gud høy-salige Konge og Herre, Kong Christian den Siette</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_N2036C">
               <persName role="composer" xml:id="_21">Johann Adolph Scheibe</persName>
               <persName role="author" xml:id="persName_8e1083ce">Peder Sparkiær</persName>
               <persName role="dedicatee" xml:id="_22">King Christian VI</persName>
            </contributor>
            <creation xml:id="creation_23_0">
               <date isodate="1746" xml:id="date_N2037D">1746</date>
               <geogName xml:id="geogName_23_0">Copenhagen,</geogName>
            </creation>
            <history xml:id="history_23_0">
               <p xml:id="idm120">When <persName xml:id="idm121" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Christian VI</persName> died in the autumn of 1746, Scheibe prepared the music for the funeral service which took place in the royal church, Christiansborg Slotskirke, on 4 October. The text for the mourning cantata was written by <persName xml:id="idm122" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Peder Sparkiær</persName> who had often provided texts for official events at the court. A large orchestra consisting of both professional as well as amateur musicians and a choir which included the boys from the Latin school of Copenhagen was brought together  – in all 80 performers participated. Some of the rehearsals, which took place in Trinitatis Kirke with the performers arranged in a half-moon at the entrance to the choir, were open to the general public. Among the audience was <persName xml:id="idm123" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Ludvig Holberg</persName>, professor at the university and a friend of Scheibe’s, standing with other music lovers between the singers and the altar. Apparently a particular passage of the cantata was so moving that according to Scheibe Holberg had to go behind the altar and hide his face sniffing in a handkerchief (see SchW C4:011 (1764)). A version of the cantata was later performed at The Musical Society (SchW B2:426); part of the text is the same though the version includes chorales and a motet. The music has not survived.</p>
            </history>
            <langUsage xml:id="langUsage_N20384">
               <language xml:id="da">Danish</language>
            </langUsage>
            <biblList xml:id="listBibl_e3e3baa0-9004-4eb6-8b5a-d42368b0c09f269">
               <head xml:id="head_N2038A">Bibliography</head>
               <bibl label="" xml:id="bibl_e6014fda">
                  <genre xml:id="genre_7bf6b312">book</genre>
                  <author xml:id="author_b87d1539">Johann Adolph Scheibe</author>
                  <annot xml:id="annot_31b8b436">
                     <p xml:id="idm132">
                        <lb xml:id="idm133"/>'Als ich nach dem Absterben Sr. Majestät Christian des VI. glorwürdigsten Andenkens, die zu dem feyerlichen Begräbnisse verfertigte Trauermusik allhier in der Trinitatiskirche einigemale öffentlich probirte: so war Holberg jedesmal ein fleißiger Zuhörer, und er ward in einer jeden Probe, bey einer gewissen sehr traurigen Stelle, die in einem stark besetzten Chore vorkam, bis zum Weinen gerührt. Die Musik, die aus mehr als achzig Personen bestand, war vor dem Eingange in dem Chor in der Figur eines halben Mondes gestellet, und Holberg stand nebst einigen andern Liebhabern zwischen der Musik und dem Altare. So bald diese ihn sehr rührende Stelle vorkam, verbarg er sich jedesmal mit dem Schnupftuche in der Hand hinter den Altar.'</p>
                  </annot>
                  <title level="m" xml:id="title_f8520d2c">Nachricht von dem Leben und den Schriften Ludwigs, Freyherrns von Holberg</title>
                  <title level="s" xml:id="title_2935c769"/>
